{
  "requirement": "After one valid judgment, the harness MUST write the subject text, submitted\ndetermination and rationale, and recorded inputs using the existing evidence\nschema. {cite}`requirements.ci.schema`\nBy default, it MUST write the subject's evidence file. With `--stdout`, it MUST\nemit a single evidence JSON object followed by a newline to stdout and MUST NOT\ncreate or change evidence files or directories. All three outcomes MUST be\nwritten to the selected destination as completed assessments; their validation\nstatus MUST follow {cite}`requirements.ci.determination`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the persistence requirement is met.** The marked implementation covers every obligation, and the tests cover the interesting cases. The one gap I found, a cite-only struct, is minor.\n\n**Implementation**\n- **Record contents.** `assess` (`src/authoring/mod.rs`, lines 450–580) builds the evidence object only after one valid `submit_judgment`. It contains the subject text under the `requirement` or `example` key, `determination` (from `Judgment::to_value`), `rationale`, and `inputs` (from `sources.recorded_inputs()`).\n  - `Judgment::to_value` (`src/judgment.rs`) emits `true`, `false`, or `{\"FIXME\":[…]}`, which are the three outcomes of the evidence schema.\n  - The submission check rejects empty rationales. Outline inputs carry only file, item, and hash.\n  - Multiple or invalid submissions never reach persistence.\n- **Default destination.** `configured_assessment` (`src/authoring/mod.rs`, lines 300–350) does the following:\n  - It creates `evidence/` and writes `evidence/<subject>.json` with a trailing newline.\n  - It sets `evidence_written` only after the write succeeds.\n  - A failed write returns an error, and the usage log stays `failed` with `evidence_written: false`.\n- **`--stdout`.** The same function takes the `EvidenceOutput::Stdout` branch, which writes one pretty-printed JSON object plus a newline to stdout and flushes. That branch never calls `create_dir_all`, `fs::write`, or any other project-file operation. Usage state is forced outside the project in `usage_state_root`, so evidence files and directories are untouched.\n- **Batch mode.** `write_record` (`src/authoring/batch.rs`) writes the same record to the same path. It uses a temp file plus `persist`, which is justified by the batch requirement's concurrent readers, not by this one. `PendingAssessment::persisted` marks the usage as written and completed.\n- **Outcomes and validation.** All three outcomes take the same write path, so false and FIXME are written as completed assessments, not errors. Validation status follows `requirements.ci.determination`; that rule is applied by the validator and cited, not reimplemented in the authoring code.\n- **Cite-only marking.** `PendingAssessment` (`src/authoring/mod.rs`, line 65) carries only a `{cite}`. It is a plain data holder with no behavior, and the behavior that persists it is marked `{impl}`. I do not count this as a real gap.\n\n**Tests**\n- **Default write for false.** `false_is_a_completed_assessment` checks that:\n  - the existing evidence file is replaced;\n  - the `false` determination and rationale are stored;\n  - inputs are non-empty and hold outline form (`item` and `hash`) without source text;\n  - usage is `completed`.\n- **Stdout.** `stdout_emits_only_evidence_json_without_changing_project_files` runs these cases:\n  - true, false, and FIXME with existing evidence;\n  - true with no `evidence/` directory, which asserts the directory is not created;\n  - verbose variants.\n\n  For each it asserts that:\n  - stdout parses as a single JSON object ending in a newline;\n  - the fields and the requirements, definition, and outline inputs are present;\n  - the existing evidence file is byte-identical.\n- **Write failure.** `failed_evidence_write_keeps_the_accepted_judgment_in_usage` replaces the evidence file with a directory. It asserts the command fails, `evidence_written` is false, and the accepted FIXME is retained in usage.\n- **No write on denial.** `explicit_untracked_reads_abort_without_another_request_or_evidence_write` asserts that no evidence is written when an assessment aborts.\n- **Batch.**\n  - The batch tests check that true, false, and FIXME records are written, and that aborted assessments leave no file while in-flight ones are preserved.\n  - The batch test `batch_fills_evidence_…` validates the written evidence through `validate_repository`.\n  - `negative_assessment_records_additional_reads_and_fails_validation` shows a false record is persisted and then reported by validation.\n- **Live provider.** `live_bedrock_authoring` exercises stdout persistence end to end. It is skipped when `CI` is set or credentials are missing.",
